::ENG4_U02_L03_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.03] Passage: Nadia pressed her palm against the old orchard gate while her brother Tomas unfolded the developer's letter. The trees behind them were bent and hollow in places, but in spring they still filled the road with white blossoms. "The offer pays every debt," Tomas said. "It gives us a future." Nadia looked past him to the rows their grandmother had planted after arriving with two suitcases and no English. "It gives us money," she answered. "That is not the same thing." Tomas folded the letter carefully, as if neatness could make the decision merciful. "Grandmother wanted us safe," he said. Nadia opened the gate and stepped inside. "She also wanted us rooted." What is Tomas's perspective? {
~A. The orchard has no connection to family history.#This ignores his reference to their grandmother.
=B. Selling the orchard can provide safety and financial relief.#Correct. Tomas connects the offer to debts and future security.
~C. The developer's letter should be destroyed.#This is not stated.
~D. The orchard should become a museum.#This is not stated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L03_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.03] Passage: Nadia looked past him to the rows their grandmother had planted after arriving with two suitcases and no English. "It gives us money," she answered. "That is not the same thing." What is Nadia's perspective? {
~A. Money and future are exactly the same.#She says they are not the same.
~B. The orchard has no meaning.#She connects it to grandmother.
=C. The orchard represents family roots and identity, not only property value.#Correct. Her perspective values inherited belonging.
~D. The trees are already gone.#The trees are still present.
}
::ENG4_U02_L03_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: Tomas folded the letter carefully, as if neatness could make the decision merciful. What does merciful suggest? {
=A. Less painful or more compassionate#Correct. The word suggests easing harm.
~B. More expensive#That is not the meaning.
~C. Completely silent#This is unrelated.
~D. Physically larger#This is unrelated.
}
::ENG4_U02_L03_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Passage: "Grandmother wanted us safe," he said. Nadia opened the gate and stepped inside. "She also wanted us rooted." Which paraphrase is most accurate? {
~A. Tomas and Nadia agree that their grandmother wanted the orchard sold.#They disagree about what her wishes mean.
~B. Nadia says grandmother wanted them to leave immediately.#This contradicts "rooted."
~C. Tomas says grandmother disliked the orchard.#This is not stated.
=D. Tomas connects grandmother with safety, while Nadia connects her with belonging to place.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U02_L03_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Passage: Tomas says the sale gives them a future, while Nadia says money is not the same thing. Which oral explanation is most coherent? {
~A. Tomas and Nadia argue because trees exist and letters are folded.#This is vague and poorly organized.
=B. Both characters have valid concerns: Tomas emphasizes survival, while Nadia emphasizes identity.#Correct
~C. The orchard is just a setting, so their views do not matter.#This misses perspective.
~D. Nadia wins the argument because she enters the gate.#This oversimplifies the conflict.
}
